<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Book of Documents</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Shangshu, Classic of History, Shujing</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Book of Documents (Shangshu) is one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/four-books-and-five-classics">Five Classics</a> of Chinese literature, a collection of speeches, proclamations and records attributed to the legendary sage kings and to rulers and ministers of the Xia, Shang and Western Zhou. Its oldest chapters, probably composed in the Western Zhou, set out the doctrine of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandate-of-heaven">Mandate of Heaven</a>. Many chapters of the transmitted text were later shown to be forgeries of the fourth century CE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/book-of-documents.jpg" alt="Book of Documents" width="320" height="157" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guwen_Shangshu_TNM.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to a later tradition, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> compiled the Book of Documents as a selection from a much larger group of documents, but the early history of the text is obscure. Writers from Confucius onwards drew on it to illustrate general principles, though several different versions seem to have been in use. It was cited increasingly in works through the 4th century BCE, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mencius">Mencius</a>, Mozi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zuo-zhuan">Zuo Zhuan</a>. Mencius and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xunzi-philosopher">Xunzi</a> refused to accept it as genuine in its entirety, in contrast to the reverence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>, when its compilation was attributed to Confucius.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The transmitted text is grouped into four sections: the semi-mythical reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yu-the-great">Yu the Great</a>, and the Xia, Shang and Zhou dynasties. The Zhou section is over half the text. Most chapters are represented as formal speeches by kings or other important figures, such as announcements, declarations made before battle, and commands to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. A speech is often introduced with the phrase Wáng ruò yuē, which scholars take to mean that the originals were prepared scripts read out by an official on the king's behalf.</p><p>The five Zhou announcements have the most archaic language and are considered by most scholars to record speeches of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-cheng-of-zhou">King Cheng of Zhou</a> and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-zhou">Duke of Zhou</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-shao">Duke of Shao</a>, his uncles, in the late 11th century BCE. They set out the doctrine of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandate-of-heaven">Mandate of Heaven</a>, which explains how the virtuous Xia became corrupt and were replaced by the Shang, who in turn were replaced by the Zhou. Most scholars believe the chapters on Yu, the Xia and the early Shang were composed later, some as late as the 4th or 3rd centuries BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Many copies were destroyed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty</a>'s burning of books. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fu-sheng">Fu Sheng</a> reconstructed 29 chapters from hidden copies at the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>, known as the Modern Script. A longer Old Script version, with some 16 additional chapters, was said to have been found in a wall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a>'s house in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a> and interpreted by his descendant Kong Anguo, but it was lost by the end of the Han dynasty.</p><p>In 317 CE Mei Ze presented to the Eastern Jin court a version of 58 chapters as Kong Anguo's text. It was widely accepted and made the official interpretation by imperial decree in 653. Doubts began in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a>, and in the 17th century Yan Ruoqu's study convinced most scholars that the added Old Script chapters were fabricated. Bamboo slips from tombs of the state of Chu, dated to around 300 BCE, quote previously unknown passages.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Book of Documents” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The doctrine of the Mandate of Heaven (tianming), set out in chapters of the <b>Book of Documents</b> attributed to the Duke of Zhou, held that Heaven entrusted rule to the virtuous and withdrew it from the wicked.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-zhou">Western Zhou · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…linked to Confucius as their editor or author: the Book of Songs, a collection of ancient poems; the <b>Book of Documents</b>, speeches and records of early kings; the Book of Rites, on ceremony and conduct; the Book of Changes, the Yijing, a manual of divination with philosophical commentaries;…</blockquote><a href="/religion/confucianism">Confucianism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Many Chinese archaeologists identify the Sanxingdui culture with the ancient kingdom of Shu, which the Shiji and the <b>Shujing</b> mention as an ally of the Zhou, who defeated the Shang.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sanxingdui-culture">Sanxingdui culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
